{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8651ad88a653dcd1", "type": "tool_result", "content": "400\tapproximately 1.08B parameters and are the only\n401\tcomponents retained at inference. The contrastive\n402\thead and distillation projectors add approximately\n403\t49M training-only parameters and are discarded after training, introducing no inference overhead. Table 1 provides the detailed parameter breakdown.\n404\t\n405\tModule\n406\tRetained at inference\n407\tVideo VAE (enc. + dec.)\n408\tAudio VAE (enc. + dec.)\n409\tSubtotal\n410\tTraining-only\n411\tContrastive head\n412\tDistill. projectors\n413\tSubtotal\n414\tTotal (training)\n415\t\n416\t#Params\n417\t704.7 M\n418\t371.6 M\n419\t1,076.3 M\n420\t20.5 M\n421\t29.0 M\n422\t49.5 M\n423\t1,125.8 M\n424\t\n425\tTable 1 Parameter counts. Training-only modules are\n426\tdiscarded at inference.\n427\t\n428\tTraining and Baselines. We organize the experiments as a progressive ablation over training objectives that\n429\tisolates the contribution of reconstruction, semantic distillation, and audio-visual contrastive learning: (i)\n430\tRecon—only the per-modality reconstruction losses, with the video and audio VAEs trained independently;\n431\t(ii) Recon+Distill—adds modality-specific semantic distillation, still training the two VAEs independently;\n432\t(iii) Recon+AVCLIP—instead adds the segment-level audio-visual contrastive loss and trains the two VAEs\n433\tjointly; and (iv) OmniVAE (full model)—trains the two VAEs jointly with reconstruction, distillation, and\n434\tcontrastive objectives enabled simultaneously. The video and audio branches of OmniVAE are initialized\n435\tfrom the Recon+Distill checkpoints, while the contrastive head is trained from scratch.\n436\tThe per-modality variants (i, ii) are trained for 250k steps on 121-frame clips, and the joint variants (iii, iv) for\n437\t82k steps on 193-frame clips with a global batch size of 256. Joint training follows a two-stage schedule that\n438\tfirst freezes the video VAE to prioritize audio reconstruction and cross-modal alignment, and then unfreezes\n439\tit to jointly optimize all objectives. Since joint training omits the audio discriminators for memory efficiency,\n440\twe briefly fine-tune the audio decoder of the AVCLIP-based variants with adversarial supervision, removing\n441\tmild electronic artifacts with negligible changes in objective metrics. The relative scales of the loss groups\n442\tfollow the contrastive-anchored loss-magnitude weighting discussed in §4.5.2. All variants use the same\n443\tvideo and audio VAE backbones, training data, and optimization settings.\n444\t\n445\t4.2\n446\t\n447\tReconstruction Evaluation\n448\t\n449\tWe first compare the reconstruction quality of different training configurations. The video VAE is evaluated\n450\ton UCF-101 [42] and Panda-70M [43] at 24 fps, 121 frames, and 256 × 256. The audio VAE is evaluated on\n451\tLibriSpeech [44] test-clean for speech, AudioSet [40] for general audio, and MUSDB18 [45] for music.\n452\tTable 2 reports the results. On the video side, all training variants remain close to the reconstruction-only\n453\tbaseline across the reconstruction metrics, indicating that the additional objectives largely preserve video\n454\treconstruction quality. Our Recon model is initialized from the official Wan2.2 VAE checkpoint and then finetuned using only reconstruction objectives. The official Wan VAEs are trained across diverse video settings\n455\tand are therefore included for reference only. On the audio side, our models build upon the open-source\n456\tHunyuanVideo-Foley VAE. Semantic distillation largely preserves reconstruction quality, while contrastive\n457\talignment leads to only a slight degradation. Even so, OmniVAE remains competitive with or outperforms\n458\tthe external audio VAEs on most reported metrics. Overall, OmniVAE retains strong reconstruction quality\n459\tacross video, speech, general audio, and music.\n460\t\n461\t7\n462\t\n463\t\f(a) Video Reconstruction\n464\t\n465\tUCF-101 / Panda-70M\n466\t\n467\tConfiguration\n468\t\n469\tPSNR ↑\n470\t\n471\tSSIM ↑\n472\t\n473\tLPIPS ↓\n474\t\n475\tL1 ↓\n476\t\n477\trFVD ↓\n478\t\n479\tExternal VAEs\n480\tWan2.1 VAE [18]\n481\tWan2.2 VAE [35]\n482\t\n483\t34.50 / 32.62\n484\t34.87 / 33.21\n485\t\n486\t0.9510 / 0.9448\n487\t0.9584 / 0.9541\n488\t\n489\t0.0201 / 0.0169\n490\t0.0177 / 0.0137\n491\t\n492\t0.0117 / 0.0130\n493\t0.0110 / 0.0120\n494\t\n495\t2.46 / 1.49\n496\t3.72 / 1.38\n497\t\n498\tOurs\n499\tRecon\n500\tRecon + Distill\n501\tRecon + AVCLIP\n502\tOmniVAE\n503\t\n504\t36.93 / 36.56\n505\t36.70 / 36.22\n506\t36.22 / 35.13\n507\t36.27 / 35.24\n508\t\n509\t0.9656 / 0.9697\n510\t0.9649 / 0.9688\n511\t0.9616 / 0.9631\n512\t0.9616 / 0.9632\n513\t\n514\t0.0102 / 0.0067\n515\t0.0107 / 0.0071\n516\t0.0115 / 0.0079\n517\t0.0113 / 0.0077\n518\t\n519\t0.0090 / 0.0086\n520\t0.0092 / 0.0089\n521\t0.0097 / 0.0098\n522\t0.0097 / 0.0098\n523\t\n524\t2.40 / 1.25\n525\t2.53 / 1.26\n526\t2.66 / 1.35\n527\t2.81 / 1.31\n528\t\n529\t(b) Audio Reconstruction\n530\tSpeech\n531\t\n532\tAudio / Music\n533\t\n534\tModel\n535\t\n536\tSIM ↑\n537\t\n538\tSTOI ↑\n539\t\n540\tP-NB ↑\n541\t\n542\tP-WB ↑\n543\t\n544\tMel ↓\n545\t\n546\tSTFT ↓\n547\t\n548\tViSQOL ↑\n549\t\n550\tExternal VAEs\n551\tHunyuanVideo-Foley VAE [7]\n552\tMMAudio VAE [6]\n553\tStable Audio 3 SAME-S [46]\n554\tStable Audio 3 SAME-L [46]\n555\t\n556\t0.9899\n557\t0.9122\n558\t0.7960\n559\t0.8755\n560\t\n561\t0.9979\n562\t0.9502\n563\t0.9139\n564\t0.9539\n565\t\n566\t4.4780\n567\t3.4931\n568\t2.8073\n569\t3.4615\n570\t\n571\t4.5337\n572\t2.9797\n573\t2.2991\n574\t3.0739\n575\t\n576\t0.52 / 0.60\n577\t1.23 / 1.36\n578\t1.46 / 1.37\n579\t1.37 / 1.22\n580\t\n581\t1.82 / 1.84\n582\t2.14 / 2.44\n583\t3.19 / 3.67\n584\t3.30 / 3.55\n585\t\n586\t4.47 / 4.45\n587\t4.42 / 4.42\n588\t3.68 / 3.99\n589\t3.76 / 4.17\n590\t\n591\tOurs\n592\tRecon\n593\tRecon + Distill\n594\tRecon + AVCLIP\n595\tOmniVAE\n596\t\n597\t0.9893\n598\t0.9893\n599\t0.9666\n600\t0.9737\n601\t\n602\t0.9981\n603\t0.9970\n604\t0.9946\n605\t0.9948\n606\t\n607\t4.4916\n608\t4.4453\n609\t4.4346\n610\t4.4400\n611\t\n612\t4.5557\n613\t4.5101\n614\t4.3638\n615\t4.4085\n616\t\n617\t0.44 / 0.50\n618\t0.56 / 0.64\n619\t0.76 / 0.75\n620\t0.74 / 0.73\n621\t\n622\t1.75 / 1.72\n623\t1.96 / 1.95\n624\t1.99 / 2.03\n625\t2.02 / 2.08\n626\t\n627\t4.55 / 4.60\n628\t4.44 / 4.42\n629\t4.03 / 4.18\n630\t4.08 / 4.31\n631\t\n632\tTable 2 Video and audio reconstruction quality. Video values follow UCF-101 / Panda-70M, while Audio / Music\n633\tvalues follow AudioSet / MUSDB18. All our video variants are initialized from Wan2.2, and our audio variants from\n634\tthe HunyuanVideo-Foley VAE. External models serve as reference points rather than training-matched baselines.\n635\t\n636\t4.3\n637\t\n638\tAudio-Video Sync Probing\n639\t\n640\tEvaluating fine-grained audio-video alignment through downstream joint generation is expensive, as each\n641\tcomparison requires training a full generative model. We therefore adopt audio-video sync probing as an\n642\tefficient and sensitive proxy. The probe predicts the temporal offset between audio and video, requiring it\n643\tto associate sound events with their visual sources at fine temporal granularity.\n644\tWe evaluate on VGGSound-Sparse (VGS-Sp),\n645\twhich contains sparse, temporally localizable\n646\tevents [8, 47, 48]. Following Synchformer, we shift\n647\taudio by 𝑁 × 0.2 seconds and train a lightweight\n648\thead to classify 21 offsets from −2 to +2 seconds.\n649\tWe report A@1, A@5, and A@1tol with a ±1-class\n650\ttolerance. Under frozen, only the classifier is\n651\ttrained; +ft also tunes the contrastive aggregators.\n652\tTable 3 shows that contrastive learning provides\n653\tthe main gain in temporal alignment, with OmniVAE performing best under both protocols.\n654\t\n655\tMethod\n656\t\n657\tA@1\n658\t\n659\tA@5\n660\t\n661\tA@1tol\n662\t\n663\tFrozen\n664\tRecon\n665\tRecon + Distill\n666\tRecon + AVCLIP\n667\tOmniVAE (ours)\n668\t\n669\t6.4\n670\t7.1\n671\t18.1\n672\t20.2\n673\t\n674\t31.5\n675\t33.0\n676\t47.8\n677\t50.1\n678\t\n679\t16.2\n680\t17.5\n681\t34.5\n682\t37.2\n683\t\n684\t+ft\n685\tRecon + ft\n686\tRecon + Distill + ft\n687\tRecon + AVCLIP + ft\n688\tOmniVAE + ft\n689\t\n690\t7.0\n691\t7.8\n692\t21.8\n693\t22.3\n694\t\n695\t32.4\n696\t33.9\n697\t52.4\n698\t53.2\n699\t\n700\t17.1\n701\t18.4\n702\t39.6\n703\t40.4\n704\t\n705\tTable 3 Sync probing on VGS-Sp (%); A@1tol uses ±1 tolerance.\n706\t\n707\t8\n708\t\n709\t\fVideo Quality\n710\t\n711\tModal Alignment\n712\t\n713\trecon\n714\tDesync\n715\t\n716\t1.0\n717\t\n718\tLSE-C\n719\t\n720\tAudio Quality\n721\t\n722\t0.28\n723\t\n724\t0.6\n725\t\n726\t1.75\n727\t\n728\t0.26\n729\t\n730\t1.50\n731\t\n732\t0.24\n733\t\n734\tAesthetic\n735\t\n736\t0.385\n737\t0.380\n738\t0.375\n739\t0.370\n740\t\n741\t0.52\n742\t\n743\t0.36\n744\t\n745\t0.50\n746\t\n747\t0.34\n748\t\n749\t1.1\n750\t\n751\tContent Enjoyment (CE)\n752\t\n753\t6.2\n754\t6.0\n755\t5.8\n756\t5.6\n757\t\n758\t4.8\n759\t4.6\n760\t\n761\tStep\n762\t\n763\t0.24\n764\t0.22\n765\t0.20\n766\t\n767\t150k\n768\t\n769\t200k\n770\t\n771\t0.7\n772\t0.5\n773\t0.4\n774\t0.250\n775\t0.225\n776\t0.200\n777\t0.175\n778\t\n779\t0.9\n780\t\n781\tContent Usefulness (CU)\n782\t\n783\t2.4\n784\t\n785\tProduction Complexity (PC)\n786\t\n787\tStep\n788\t\n789\t150k\n790\t\n791\t200k\n792\t\n793\tProduction Quality (PQ)\n794\t6.25\n795\t6.00\n796\t\n797\t2.2\n798\t100k\n799\t\n800\tSpeech WER\n801\t\n802\t6.50\n803\t\n804\t2.3\n805\t50k\n806\t\n807\tMotion Score\n808\t\n809\t0.6\n810\t\n811\t1.0\n812\t\n813\t1.2\n814\t\n815\t3.0\n816\t\n817\tText-Audio Sim\n818\t\n819\tFréchet Distance\n820\t\n821\t1.3\n822\t\n823\t3.5\n824\t\n825\t100k\n826\t\n827\tVideo-Audio Sim\n828\t\n829\tManiQA\n830\t\n831\tKL Divergence\n832\t\n833\t4.0\n834\t\n835\t50k\n836\t\n837\trecon_distill_avclip\n838\t\n839\tMusiQ\n840\t\n841\tInception Score\n842\t\n843\tAudio Aesthetics\n844\t\n845\trecon_avclip\n846\t\n847\t2.00\n848\t\n849\t0.8\n850\t\n851\t4.4\n852\t\n853\trecon_distill\n854\t\n855\t50k\n856\t\n857\t100k\n858\t\n859\tStep\n860\t\n861\t150k\n862\t\n863\t200k\n864\t\n865\t50k\n866\t\n867\t100k\n868\t\n869\tStep\n870\t\n871\t150k\n872\t\n873\t200k\n874\t\n875\tFigure 2 Evaluation metrics of T2AV models trained on the four VAE configurations, tracked across training steps.\n876\tRows, top to bottom: cross-modal alignment (Desync, LSE-C, Video-Audio Sim, Text-Audio Sim), video quality (Aesthetic, MusiQ, ManiQA, Motion Score), AudioBox aesthetics [49] (Content Enjoyment, Content Usefulness, Production\n877\tComplexity, Production Quality), and audio quality (IS, KL Divergence, Fréchet Distance, Speech WER). The AVCLIPequipped configurations consistently lead on all cross-modal alignment metrics, both semantic objectives improve audio\n878\tquality, and the full OmniVAE (recon_distill_avclip) achieves the best overall results.\n879\t\n880\t4.4\n881\t\n882\tText-to-Audio-Video Generation\n883\t\n884\tBenchmark. We evaluate T2AV generation on Verse-Bench [50] using the unified T2AV prompts introduced\n885\tin MOVA [34]. Verse-Bench comprises three subsets: Set1-I (205 samples sourced from still images), Set2-V\n886\t(295 samples sourced from web videos), and Set3-TED (100 TED-talk samples used for lip-sync evaluation).\n887\tBecause our T2AV models generate at 256×256 resolution, faces are often too small for SyncNet [17] to detect,\n888\tleaving only a small number of samples for which a lip-sync score can be computed. We therefore use an\n889\tLLM to rewrite each Set3 visual prompt into a medium close-up or close-up shot while preserving its original\n890\tintent and speech content, so that faces occupy a larger fraction of the frame. All baselines are evaluated on\n891\tthe rewritten prompts for fair comparison. Set3 results under this protocol are not directly comparable to\n892\tthose reported using the original Verse-Bench prompts.\n893\tResults. Figure 2 compares the four VAE configurations throughout T2AV training. For a checkpointindependent quantitative comparison, Table 4 reports the mean over the final three checkpoints (180k, 190k,\n894\tand 200k) under CFG = 5, using the same checkpoints for every configuration. On cross-modal alignment,\n895\tDesync measures fine-grained temporal synchronization and LSE-C measures lip synchronization, while\n896\tVideo-Audio Sim and Text-Audio Sim evaluate overall semantic consistency. The AVCLIP-based variants\n897\tconsistently improve both groups of metrics, with OmniVAE achieving the strongest overall alignment. On\n898\taudio quality, the AudioBox scores jointly assess content and production quality, IS, KL divergence, and\n899\tFréchet distance evaluate non-speech audio generation, and WER measures speech intelligibility. Both semantic distillation and AVCLIP improve these metrics individually, while their combination achieves the\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "6fd9fdf1-b774-46c0-b355-0709c229ad96", "uuid": "a7f0b072-04a2-4e1e-ac3c-004f85b8d2eb", "timestamp": "2026-07-28T08:16:55.592Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/omnivae.txt", "content": "approximately 1.08B parameters and are the only\ncomponents retained at inference. The contrastive\nhead and distillation projectors add approximately\n49M training-only parameters and are discarded after training, introducing no inference overhead. Table 1 provides the detailed parameter breakdown.\n\nModule\nRetained at inference\nVideo VAE (enc. + dec.)\nAudio VAE (enc. + dec.)\nSubtotal\nTraining-only\nContrastive head\nDistill. projectors\nSubtotal\nTotal (training)\n\n#Params\n704.7 M\n371.6 M\n1,076.3 M\n20.5 M\n29.0 M\n49.5 M\n1,125.8 M\n\nTable 1 Parameter counts. Training-only modules are\ndiscarded at inference.\n\nTraining and Baselines. We organize the experiments as a progressive ablation over training objectives that\nisolates the contribution of reconstruction, semantic distillation, and audio-visual contrastive learning: (i)\nRecon—only the per-modality reconstruction losses, with the video and audio VAEs trained independently;\n(ii) Recon+Distill—adds modality-specific semantic distillation, still training the two VAEs independently;\n(iii) Recon+AVCLIP—instead adds the segment-level audio-visual contrastive loss and trains the two VAEs\njointly; and (iv) OmniVAE (full model)—trains the two VAEs jointly with reconstruction, distillation, and\ncontrastive objectives enabled simultaneously. The video and audio branches of OmniVAE are initialized\nfrom the Recon+Distill checkpoints, while the contrastive head is trained from scratch.\nThe per-modality variants (i, ii) are trained for 250k steps on 121-frame clips, and the joint variants (iii, iv) for\n82k steps on 193-frame clips with a global batch size of 256. Joint training follows a two-stage schedule that\nfirst freezes the video VAE to prioritize audio reconstruction and cross-modal alignment, and then unfreezes\nit to jointly optimize all objectives. Since joint training omits the audio discriminators for memory efficiency,\nwe briefly fine-tune the audio decoder of the AVCLIP-based variants with adversarial supervision, removing\nmild electronic artifacts with negligible changes in objective metrics. The relative scales of the loss groups\nfollow the contrastive-anchored loss-magnitude weighting discussed in §4.5.2. All variants use the same\nvideo and audio VAE backbones, training data, and optimization settings.\n\n4.2\n\nReconstruction Evaluation\n\nWe first compare the reconstruction quality of different training configurations. The video VAE is evaluated\non UCF-101 [42] and Panda-70M [43] at 24 fps, 121 frames, and 256 × 256. The audio VAE is evaluated on\nLibriSpeech [44] test-clean for speech, AudioSet [40] for general audio, and MUSDB18 [45] for music.\nTable 2 reports the results. On the video side, all training variants remain close to the reconstruction-only\nbaseline across the reconstruction metrics, indicating that the additional objectives largely preserve video\nreconstruction quality. Our Recon model is initialized from the official Wan2.2 VAE checkpoint and then finetuned using only reconstruction objectives. The official Wan VAEs are trained across diverse video settings\nand are therefore included for reference only. On the audio side, our models build upon the open-source\nHunyuanVideo-Foley VAE. Semantic distillation largely preserves reconstruction quality, while contrastive\nalignment leads to only a slight degradation. Even so, OmniVAE remains competitive with or outperforms\nthe external audio VAEs on most reported metrics. Overall, OmniVAE retains strong reconstruction quality\nacross video, speech, general audio, and music.\n\n7\n\n\f(a) Video Reconstruction\n\nUCF-101 / Panda-70M\n\nConfiguration\n\nPSNR ↑\n\nSSIM ↑\n\nLPIPS ↓\n\nL1 ↓\n\nrFVD ↓\n\nExternal VAEs\nWan2.1 VAE [18]\nWan2.2 VAE [35]\n\n34.50 / 32.62\n34.87 / 33.21\n\n0.9510 / 0.9448\n0.9584 / 0.9541\n\n0.0201 / 0.0169\n0.0177 / 0.0137\n\n0.0117 / 0.0130\n0.0110 / 0.0120\n\n2.46 / 1.49\n3.72 / 1.38\n\nOurs\nRecon\nRecon + Distill\nRecon + AVCLIP\nOmniVAE\n\n36.93 / 36.56\n36.70 / 36.22\n36.22 / 35.13\n36.27 / 35.24\n\n0.9656 / 0.9697\n0.9649 / 0.9688\n0.9616 / 0.9631\n0.9616 / 0.9632\n\n0.0102 / 0.0067\n0.0107 / 0.0071\n0.0115 / 0.0079\n0.0113 / 0.0077\n\n0.0090 / 0.0086\n0.0092 / 0.0089\n0.0097 / 0.0098\n0.0097 / 0.0098\n\n2.40 / 1.25\n2.53 / 1.26\n2.66 / 1.35\n2.81 / 1.31\n\n(b) Audio Reconstruction\nSpeech\n\nAudio / Music\n\nModel\n\nSIM ↑\n\nSTOI ↑\n\nP-NB ↑\n\nP-WB ↑\n\nMel ↓\n\nSTFT ↓\n\nViSQOL ↑\n\nExternal VAEs\nHunyuanVideo-Foley VAE [7]\nMMAudio VAE [6]\nStable Audio 3 SAME-S [46]\nStable Audio 3 SAME-L [46]\n\n0.9899\n0.9122\n0.7960\n0.8755\n\n0.9979\n0.9502\n0.9139\n0.9539\n\n4.4780\n3.4931\n2.8073\n3.4615\n\n4.5337\n2.9797\n2.2991\n3.0739\n\n0.52 / 0.60\n1.23 / 1.36\n1.46 / 1.37\n1.37 / 1.22\n\n1.82 / 1.84\n2.14 / 2.44\n3.19 / 3.67\n3.30 / 3.55\n\n4.47 / 4.45\n4.42 / 4.42\n3.68 / 3.99\n3.76 / 4.17\n\nOurs\nRecon\nRecon + Distill\nRecon + AVCLIP\nOmniVAE\n\n0.9893\n0.9893\n0.9666\n0.9737\n\n0.9981\n0.9970\n0.9946\n0.9948\n\n4.4916\n4.4453\n4.4346\n4.4400\n\n4.5557\n4.5101\n4.3638\n4.4085\n\n0.44 / 0.50\n0.56 / 0.64\n0.76 / 0.75\n0.74 / 0.73\n\n1.75 / 1.72\n1.96 / 1.95\n1.99 / 2.03\n2.02 / 2.08\n\n4.55 / 4.60\n4.44 / 4.42\n4.03 / 4.18\n4.08 / 4.31\n\nTable 2 Video and audio reconstruction quality. Video values follow UCF-101 / Panda-70M, while Audio / Music\nvalues follow AudioSet / MUSDB18. All our video variants are initialized from Wan2.2, and our audio variants from\nthe HunyuanVideo-Foley VAE. External models serve as reference points rather than training-matched baselines.\n\n4.3\n\nAudio-Video Sync Probing\n\nEvaluating fine-grained audio-video alignment through downstream joint generation is expensive, as each\ncomparison requires training a full generative model. We therefore adopt audio-video sync probing as an\nefficient and sensitive proxy. The probe predicts the temporal offset between audio and video, requiring it\nto associate sound events with their visual sources at fine temporal granularity.\nWe evaluate on VGGSound-Sparse (VGS-Sp),\nwhich contains sparse, temporally localizable\nevents [8, 47, 48]. Following Synchformer, we shift\naudio by 𝑁 × 0.2 seconds and train a lightweight\nhead to classify 21 offsets from −2 to +2 seconds.\nWe report A@1, A@5, and A@1tol with a ±1-class\ntolerance. Under frozen, only the classifier is\ntrained; +ft also tunes the contrastive aggregators.\nTable 3 shows that contrastive learning provides\nthe main gain in temporal alignment, with OmniVAE performing best under both protocols.\n\nMethod\n\nA@1\n\nA@5\n\nA@1tol\n\nFrozen\nRecon\nRecon + Distill\nRecon + AVCLIP\nOmniVAE (ours)\n\n6.4\n7.1\n18.1\n20.2\n\n31.5\n33.0\n47.8\n50.1\n\n16.2\n17.5\n34.5\n37.2\n\n+ft\nRecon + ft\nRecon + Distill + ft\nRecon + AVCLIP + ft\nOmniVAE + ft\n\n7.0\n7.8\n21.8\n22.3\n\n32.4\n33.9\n52.4\n53.2\n\n17.1\n18.4\n39.6\n40.4\n\nTable 3 Sync probing on VGS-Sp (%); A@1tol uses ±1 tolerance.\n\n8\n\n\fVideo Quality\n\nModal Alignment\n\nrecon\nDesync\n\n1.0\n\nLSE-C\n\nAudio Quality\n\n0.28\n\n0.6\n\n1.75\n\n0.26\n\n1.50\n\n0.24\n\nAesthetic\n\n0.385\n0.380\n0.375\n0.370\n\n0.52\n\n0.36\n\n0.50\n\n0.34\n\n1.1\n\nContent Enjoyment (CE)\n\n6.2\n6.0\n5.8\n5.6\n\n4.8\n4.6\n\nStep\n\n0.24\n0.22\n0.20\n\n150k\n\n200k\n\n0.7\n0.5\n0.4\n0.250\n0.225\n0.200\n0.175\n\n0.9\n\nContent Usefulness (CU)\n\n2.4\n\nProduction Complexity (PC)\n\nStep\n\n150k\n\n200k\n\nProduction Quality (PQ)\n6.25\n6.00\n\n2.2\n100k\n\nSpeech WER\n\n6.50\n\n2.3\n50k\n\nMotion Score\n\n0.6\n\n1.0\n\n1.2\n\n3.0\n\nText-Audio Sim\n\nFréchet Distance\n\n1.3\n\n3.5\n\n100k\n\nVideo-Audio Sim\n\nManiQA\n\nKL Divergence\n\n4.0\n\n50k\n\nrecon_distill_avclip\n\nMusiQ\n\nInception Score\n\nAudio Aesthetics\n\nrecon_avclip\n\n2.00\n\n0.8\n\n4.4\n\nrecon_distill\n\n50k\n\n100k\n\nStep\n\n150k\n\n200k\n\n50k\n\n100k\n\nStep\n\n150k\n\n200k\n\nFigure 2 Evaluation metrics of T2AV models trained on the four VAE configurations, tracked across training steps.\nRows, top to bottom: cross-modal alignment (Desync, LSE-C, Video-Audio Sim, Text-Audio Sim), video quality (Aesthetic, MusiQ, ManiQA, Motion Score), AudioBox aesthetics [49] (Content Enjoyment, Content Usefulness, Production\nComplexity, Production Quality), and audio quality (IS, KL Divergence, Fréchet Distance, Speech WER). The AVCLIPequipped configurations consistently lead on all cross-modal alignment metrics, both semantic objectives improve audio\nquality, and the full OmniVAE (recon_distill_avclip) achieves the best overall results.\n\n4.4\n\nText-to-Audio-Video Generation\n\nBenchmark. We evaluate T2AV generation on Verse-Bench [50] using the unified T2AV prompts introduced\nin MOVA [34]. Verse-Bench comprises three subsets: Set1-I (205 samples sourced from still images), Set2-V\n(295 samples sourced from web videos), and Set3-TED (100 TED-talk samples used for lip-sync evaluation).\nBecause our T2AV models generate at 256×256 resolution, faces are often too small for SyncNet [17] to detect,\nleaving only a small number of samples for which a lip-sync score can be computed. We therefore use an\nLLM to rewrite each Set3 visual prompt into a medium close-up or close-up shot while preserving its original\nintent and speech content, so that faces occupy a larger fraction of the frame. All baselines are evaluated on\nthe rewritten prompts for fair comparison. Set3 results under this protocol are not directly comparable to\nthose reported using the original Verse-Bench prompts.\nResults. Figure 2 compares the four VAE configurations throughout T2AV training. For a checkpointindependent quantitative comparison, Table 4 reports the mean over the final three checkpoints (180k, 190k,\nand 200k) under CFG = 5, using the same checkpoints for every configuration. On cross-modal alignment,\nDesync measures fine-grained temporal synchronization and LSE-C measures lip synchronization, while\nVideo-Audio Sim and Text-Audio Sim evaluate overall semantic consistency. The AVCLIP-based variants\nconsistently improve both groups of metrics, with OmniVAE achieving the strongest overall alignment. On\naudio quality, the AudioBox scores jointly assess content and production quality, IS, KL divergence, and\nFréchet distance evaluate non-speech audio generation, and WER measures speech intelligibility. Both semantic distillation and AVCLIP improve these metrics individually, while their combination achieves the", "numLines": 500, "startLine": 400, "totalLines": 1338}}}
