{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-ae9102adbc9ae6b4", "type": "tool_result", "content": "900\tstrongest overall audio performance. On video quality, AVCLIP improves visual aesthetics, while semantic\n901\tdistillation yields gains on MusiQ and ManiQA; motion quality remains broadly comparable across configurations. Together, these results show that AVCLIP improves both cross-modal alignment and audio genera-\n902\t\n903\t9\n904\t\n905\t\fCross-Modal Alignment\n906\tConfiguration\n907\t\n908\tDeSync ↓\n909\t\n910\tLSE-C ↑\n911\t\n912\tVideo-Audio Sim ↑\n913\t\n914\tText-Audio Sim ↑\n915\t\n916\tRecon\n917\tRecon + Distill\n918\tRecon + AVCLIP\n919\tOmniVAE\n920\t\n921\t0.884\n922\t0.814\n923\t0.576\n924\t0.570\n925\t\n926\t1.479\n927\t1.450\n928\t1.970\n929\t2.093\n930\t\n931\t0.254\n932\t0.256\n933\t0.257\n934\t0.274\n935\t\n936\t0.201\n937\t0.227\n938\t0.225\n939\t0.246\n940\t\n941\tConfiguration\n942\t\n943\tAesthetic ↑\n944\t\n945\tMusiQ ↑\n946\t\n947\tManiQA ↑\n948\t\n949\tMotion Score ↑\n950\t\n951\tRecon\n952\tRecon + Distill\n953\tRecon + AVCLIP\n954\tOmniVAE\n955\t\n956\t0.369\n957\t0.373\n958\t0.382\n959\t0.381\n960\t\n961\t0.503\n962\t0.523\n963\t0.503\n964\t0.515\n965\t\n966\t0.338\n967\t0.359\n968\t0.341\n969\t0.355\n970\t\n971\t0.556\n972\t0.495\n973\t0.533\n974\t0.456\n975\t\n976\tConfiguration\n977\t\n978\tIS ↑\n979\t\n980\tKL ↓\n981\t\n982\tFD ↓\n983\t\n984\tWER ↓\n985\t\n986\tRecon\n987\tRecon + Distill\n988\tRecon + AVCLIP\n989\tOmniVAE\n990\t\n991\t3.041\n992\t3.604\n993\t3.598\n994\t4.001\n995\t\n996\t1.279\n997\t1.258\n998\t1.231\n999\t1.140\n1000\t\n1001\t1.029\n1002\t0.959\n1003\t0.932\n1004\t0.875\n1005\t\n1006\t0.243\n1007\t0.205\n1008\t0.172\n1009\t0.168\n1010\t\n1011\tConfiguration\n1012\t\n1013\tCE ↑\n1014\t\n1015\tCU ↑\n1016\t\n1017\tPC ↓\n1018\t\n1019\tPQ ↑\n1020\t\n1021\tRecon\n1022\tRecon + Distill\n1023\tRecon + AVCLIP\n1024\tOmniVAE\n1025\t\n1026\t4.625\n1027\t4.533\n1028\t4.666\n1029\t4.826\n1030\t\n1031\t5.855\n1032\t5.677\n1033\t5.894\n1034\t6.119\n1035\t\n1036\t2.282\n1037\t2.232\n1038\t2.237\n1039\t2.259\n1040\t\n1041\t6.328\n1042\t6.060\n1043\t6.219\n1044\t6.406\n1045\t\n1046\tVideo Quality\n1047\t\n1048\tAudio Quality\n1049\t\n1050\tAudioBox Aesthetics\n1051\t\n1052\tTable 4 T2AV generation results averaged over the final three checkpoints (180k, 190k, and 200k) with CFG = 5. All\n1053\tmetrics are aggregated over the supported Verse-Bench subsets; LSE-C is evaluated on the face-detectable Set3 samples.\n1054\tCE, CU, PC, and PQ denote Content Enjoyment, Content Usefulness, Production Complexity, and Production Quality,\n1055\trespectively. Bold indicates the best result for each metric.\n1056\t\n1057\ttion quality, while semantic distillation further strengthens modality-specific generation quality. Combining\n1058\tthe two objectives yields complementary gains and the strongest overall performance.\n1059\t\n1060\t4.5\n1061\t\n1062\tAblation Study\n1063\t\n1064\tWe ablate two key designs: the temporal granularity of contrastive learning (§4.5.1) and the loss-balancing\n1065\tstrategy (§4.5.2). We evaluate alignment using sync probing for the former and segment-retrieval accuracy\n1066\tfor the latter, while also reporting reconstruction quality.\n1067\t\n1068\t10\n1069\t\n1070\t\f4.5.1\n1071\t\n1072\tContrastive Pool Size and Temporal Granularity\n1073\t\n1074\tThe contrastive pool is determined by the video frame\n1075\trate and clip duration, while the segment length controls temporal granularity. As shown in Table 5, using a\n1076\thigher frame rate together with finer temporal segments,\n1077\tor increasing the clip duration, substantially improves\n1078\tsync probing accuracy, demonstrating the importance\n1079\tof a sufficiently large negative pool. By contrast, performance is relatively stable across segment lengths. We\n1080\ttherefore use 24 fps, 8-second clips, and 0.17-second segments in the main experiments, which provide a large\n1081\tnegative pool at the finest evaluated temporal granularity.\n1082\t\n1083\t4.5.2\n1084\t\n1085\tFPS\n1086\t\n1087\tClip\n1088\t\n1089\tSegment\n1090\t\n1091\tA@1 (%)\n1092\t\n1093\t8\n1094\t24\n1095\t24\n1096\t24\n1097\t24\n1098\t24\n1099\t\n1100\t8s\n1101\t—\n1102\t2s\n1103\t8s\n1104\t8s\n1105\t8s\n1106\t\n1107\t0.50 s\n1108\t0.17 s\n1109\t0.17 s\n1110\t0.17 s\n1111\t0.34 s\n1112\t0.50 s\n1113\t\n1114\t19.0\n1115\t7.0\n1116\t6.0\n1117\t30.0\n1118\t27.0\n1119\t29.0\n1120\t\n1121\tTable 5 Ablation of contrastive pool size and temporal granularity. A@1 is measured using the VGS-Sp\n1122\ttemporal-offset probe in Table 3. The dash denotes\n1123\tthe reconstruction-only baseline without contrastive\n1124\ttraining.\n1125\t\n1126\tBalancing Reconstruction and Cross-Modal Alignment\n1127\t\n1128\tJoint training combines video reconstruction, audio reconstruction, and contrastive alignment losses with\n1129\tsubstantially different scales. Without appropriate balancing, the dense reconstruction objectives can overwhelm the comparatively sparse alignment signal. We therefore compare three strategies for balancing these\n1130\tloss groups: loss-magnitude weighting, gradient-based weighting using the last encoder layer as a proxy,\n1131\tand uncertainty-based weighting [51].\n1132\tBecause all strategies use the same contrastive setup, we evaluate alignment through segment retrieval. Intra A@48 retrieves the paired segment within the same video, whereas Overall A@64 additionally includes\n1133\tnegatives sampled from other videos in the batch. As shown in Table 6, all three strategies achieve comparable reconstruction quality, but loss-magnitude weighting performs substantially better on both retrieval\n1134\tmetrics. We therefore adopt it for joint training.\n1135\tBalancing strategy\n1136\tLoss-magnitude (ours)\n1137\tGradNorm (last-layer proxy)\n1138\tUncertainty-based adaptive\n1139\t\n1140\tSegment retrieval\n1141\t\n1142\tAudio recon.\n1143\t\n1144\tVideo recon.\n1145\t\n1146\tIntra A@48\n1147\t\n1148\tOverall A@64\n1149\t\n1150\tSTOI\n1151\t\n1152\tPESQ-WB\n1153\t\n1154\tPESQ-NB\n1155\t\n1156\tPSNR\n1157\t\n1158\t0.51\n1159\t0.44\n1160\t0.29\n1161\t\n1162\t0.24\n1163\t0.17\n1164\t0.13\n1165\t\n1166\t0.994\n1167\t0.995\n1168\t0.994\n1169\t\n1170\t4.41\n1171\t4.42\n1172\t4.45\n1173\t\n1174\t4.38\n1175\t4.40\n1176\t4.41\n1177\t\n1178\t34.1\n1179\t35.4\n1180\t33.4\n1181\t\n1182\tTable 6 Ablation of loss-balancing strategies. Segment retrieval measures fine-grained alignment, while STOI, PESQ,\n1183\tand PSNR measure reconstruction quality.\n1184\t\n1185\t5\n1186\t\n1187\tConclusion\n1188\t\n1189\tWe presented OmniVAE, a jointly trained audio-video VAE that embeds modality-specific semantics and finegrained cross-modal correspondence into the latent representations. OmniVAE augments standard reconstruction training with segment-level audio-video contrastive learning and modality-specific semantic distillation. Our experiments show that the two objectives provide complementary benefits. Contrastive learning substantially improves fine-grained audio-video alignment, while semantic distillation strengthens the\n1190\tlearnability of the modality-specific representations. These enhanced representations enable downstream\n1191\ttext-to-audio-video generation models to achieve better generation quality and more accurate cross-modal\n1192\tsynchronization without materially compromising reconstruction quality. Taken together, these findings\n1193\thighlight the importance of incorporating both modality-specific semantic structure and cross-modal correspondence directly into learned representations, providing a stronger foundation for unified multimodal\n1194\t\n1195\t11\n1196\t\n1197\t\fgeneration. Future work should explore more effective methods for modality-specific and cross-modal semantic learning and validate their benefits at larger scales.\n1198\t\n1199\tReferences\n1200\t[1] Diederik P. Kingma and Max Welling. Auto-encoding variational bayes. In International Conference on Learning\n1201\tRepresentations, 2014. URL https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.6114.\n1202\t[2] Jingfeng Yao, Bin Yang, and Xinggang Wang. Reconstruction vs. generation: Taming optimization dilemma in latent\n1203\tdiffusion models. arXiv preprint arXiv:2501.01423, 2025. URL https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.01423.\n1204\t[3] Boyang Zheng, Nanye Ma, Shengbang Tong, and Saining Xie. Diffusion transformers with representation autoencoders, 2025. URL https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11690.\n1205\t[4] Xin Zhang, Dong Zhang, Shimin Li, Yaqian Zhou, and Xipeng Qiu. SpeechTokenizer: Unified speech tokenizer\n1206\tfor speech large language models. In International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR), 2024. URL https:\n1207\t//arxiv.org/abs/2308.16692.\n1208\t[5] OpenMOSS Team. MOSS-Audio-Tokenizer: Scaling audio tokenizers for future audio foundation models, 2026.\n1209\tURL https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.10934.\n1210\t[6] Ho Kei Cheng, Masato Ishii, Akio Hayakawa, Takashi Shibuya, Alexander Schwing, and Yuki Mitsufuji. MMAudio:\n1211\tTaming multimodal joint training for high-quality video-to-audio synthesis. In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition, pages 28901–28911, 2025. doi: 10.1109/CVPR52734.2025.02691.\n1212\t[7] Sizhe Shan, Qiulin Li, Yutao Cui, Miles Yang, Yuehai Wang, Qun Yang, Jin Zhou, and Zhao Zhong. Hunyuanvideofoley: Multimodal diffusion with representation alignment for high-fidelity foley audio generation, 2025. URL\n1213\thttps://arxiv.org/abs/2508.16930.\n1214\t[8] Vladimir Iashin, Weidi Xie, Esa Rahtu, and Andrew Zisserman. Synchformer: Efficient synchronization from sparse\n1215\tcues. In ICASSP 2024 - 2024 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing, pages 5325–5329,\n1216\t2024. doi: 10.1109/ICASSP48485.2024.10448489.\n1217\t[9] Aaron van den Oord, Yazhe Li, and Oriol Vinyals. Representation learning with contrastive predictive coding, 